##


inlined nav_arrow_theme {
    "color" : golden_color;
    "margin-top" : "-40px";
    "text-decoration" : "none";
    main_font;
}

GDSL_acorn {
    standard_header;
    top_bar("/GDSL_acorn");
    div {
        outer_page_theme;
        div {
            inner_page_theme;

            div {
                "display" : "flex";
                "flex-direction" : "row";
                "justify-content" : "space-between";
                "width" : "100%";

                link "<- GDSL" {"href" : "/GDSL"; nav_arrow_theme;}
                link "Core ->" {"href" : "/GDSL_core"; nav_arrow_theme;}
            }

            section_header("Acorn");
            text "Acorn is the memory model for GDSL, it's the (current) lowest level, and it can also refer to the experimental ISA/bytecode" {post_text_theme;}
            text "Acorn is the name of a character in Golden, an orphaned child. I chose that name because the other child was Pip which is more contested in the tech space. Also, because Acorns are the seeds of things so it fits both ways." {post_text_theme;}
            link "source" {"href" : "https://github.com/FirTheMouse/GDSL/blob/main/mixos-acorn/util/Acorn-Type.hpp"; "cursor" : "pointer"; post_text_theme;}

            section_header("QCol");
            text "At its core, Acorn is really just ranges of bytes. The QCol is about as primitive as it gets, just storage, length, and capacity." {post_text_theme;}
            text "Derived from the QCol is the QString and QCellCol. Both are just forms of QCol that specialize their storage for chars and CCols respectively." {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("CCol");
            text "The CCol is the next step up from the QCol, it adds the concept of things. Where a QCol is just some range, a CCol has element_size and a tag that lets it become an array of things." {post_text_theme;}
            text "It also has Index and Hash, which are used by the QCellCol and as scratch space otherwise." {post_text_theme;}
            text "Live is more important, it's actually a general locking system with reader and writer locks crammed into a byte. It used to just be a flag for recycling." {post_text_theme;}
            text "Then there's gen and cachelevel, cachelevel is unused, gen is incremented on each recycle so staleness can be detected." {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("QCellCol");
            text "A specialization of QCol for storing CCols, this is Acorn's map when used in combination with a col." {post_text_theme;}
            text "Each CCol, or cell, uses its index to point into a Col, its hash for lookup, and its storage and tag for the key so it can be recovered for printing or collision detection." {post_text_theme;}
            text "Originally it was just linear search, but I upgraded it to be a robin hood style map." {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Col");
            text "The Col is one of the two core things in Acorn, it adds labels, free list, cells, and heterogeneity." {post_text_theme;}
            text "Label is just a QString." {post_text_theme;}
            text "The free list is a g_lib (my C++ standard library) list, it is likely it will be replaced with a QCol in the future. Its purpose is to store which slots in the column have been recycled." {post_text_theme;}
            text "Cells is a QCellCol maintained by the Col, it provides a secondary lookup method other than just indexes, using keys. It's also how slots can be labeled." {post_text_theme;}
            text "Heterogeneous is a flag that marks if a Col is storing homogenous data like an int or elements with a layout like a Node (think like a struct in C)." {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Ptr");
            text "Ptr is the other core thing in Acorn, it plays many, many roles." {post_text_theme;}
            text "A Ptr is a 32 byte structure composed of a void pointer cache, one byte cachelevel, one byte specialization, and two bytes gen. Then five levels." {post_text_theme;}
            text "Specialization is currently just used for marking if a Ptr is dead or not, but it's open for future extension." {post_text_theme;}
            text "Gen is for tracking Ptr relevance when the slot it refers to gets recycled." {post_text_theme;}
            text "Cache is used to accelerate Ptr resolution when we know the real storage won't change. The cachelevels are:" {post_text_theme;}
            text "  0=no cache (full resolution)" {post_text_theme;}
            text "  1=sidx valid, cache is a col" {post_text_theme;}
            text "  2=idx valid, cache is a ColCol" {post_text_theme;}
            text "  3=pool valid, cache is a ColColCol" {post_text_theme;}
            text "  4=subunit valid, cache is a PtrColColCol" {post_text_theme;}
            text "Most Ptrs are cachelevel 3, because subunits use one level of indirection so that their Ptrs can remain stable even as the count of subunits changes." {post_text_theme;}
            text "Cache can be swapped out to get Region and Zone as levels, but I haven't done that anywhere yet." {post_text_theme;}
        }
    }
}

##
    register GDSL_core;
    "GDSL_core" {
        in htmlemiting {
            subemit("body",false,"","");
            string old_src = ctx.source;
            ctx.source = "<link rel='icon' type='image/png' href='/web/goldensystems/files/favicon.png'>"+old_src;
        }
    }
##

GDSL_core {
    standard_header;
    top_bar("/GDSL_core");
    div {
        outer_page_theme;
        div {
            inner_page_theme;

            div {
                "display" : "flex";
                "flex-direction" : "row";
                "justify-content" : "space-between";
                "width" : "100%";

                link "<- Acorn" {"href" : "/GDSL_acorn"; nav_arrow_theme;}
                link "Compiler ->" {"href" : "/GDSL_compiler"; nav_arrow_theme;}
            }

            section_header("Core");
            text "The Core is where a lot of Acorn's model gets furnished. It adds a bunch of key elements, registers the core types, contains Units and Subunits, and much more."  {post_text_theme;}
            link "source" {"href" : "https://github.com/FirTheMouse/GDSL/blob/main/mixos-acorn/Acorn-Core.hpp"; "cursor" : "pointer"; post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Hierarchy");
            text "ColCol and ColColCol are both just Cols of Cols, also known as pools and subunits." {post_text_theme;}
            text "When a Ptr is resolved it goes through a sequence starting at its cachelevel:" {post_text_theme;}
            text "unit -> subunit -> pool -> column -> slot" {post_text_theme;}
            text "unit|subunit|pool|column|slot" {post_text_theme;}
            text "4|3|2|1|0" {post_text_theme;}
            text "These are what the cache/field levels mean. So a 'level 3 Ptr' just means it knows its location up to the subunit level" {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Layout");
            text "Layouts are a group of Cols describing how to interpret a heterogeneous Col. The layout I'm using isn't fixed right now, and it's something that's very prone to change in the future."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Currently its just offsets, labels, tags, subtags, sizes, subsizes, and ptrs. Plus an overloads column which is of a struct combining Ptrs and a type (very subject to change)."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Layouts let a Col be interpreted by its tag, it's how later structures like Node and Value are constructed while still being just Ptrs."  {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Handler");
            text "A handler is a function which accepts a Context object."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Stages are swappable groups of handlers."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "The handlers are stored in the first pool, each row is a stage."  {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Types");
            text "Types in GDSL are just a registered unsigned integer (uint32_t) id, so for instance Ptr might be type 2, int might be type 18, identifier could be 38, etc..."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Each type has its own Col in the handler pool, with slots for every stage."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Storeable types (like int, string, float) have 3 ids registered to them, the normal id, prefix id, and suffix id, nothing actually holds these ids, they are only used for handler storage."  {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Subunit");
            text "A Subunit is a Col denoting persistable group of ColCols (pools)."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "They are stored with a level of indirection, meaning that the addition of more subunits does not invalidate caches held by Ptrs at level 3."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "They can be saved, loaded, and bounced (save and remove from storage), and most database operations use subunits at their highest level."  {post_text_theme;}
            text ".gsu is the file handle they use."  {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Unit");
            text "A Unit is a self-contained process, database, compiler, and much more. They contain and manage the Acorn memory model through subunits."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "The first Subunit in a Unit is called Types and contains the handler and layout pools, alongside all the storage for compiler types. Types is often copied from the global template Subunit on Unit construction."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Units have their own ids, threads, logging, and other equipment necessary for a process."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "All the methods that allow the Acorn memory model to be operated on as a compiler or database live here."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Units also have a system called snapshotting for saving backward compatible versions of the memory model."  {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Node");
            text "A Node is an instance of something, it has position, relation to other nodes, type, name, and reference to a value."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Nodes are what handlers are typically called on, this also gives them behaviour as a side effect of their type."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "A graph of nodes is called a nodenet, traversal of a nodenet is called a program."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Node is a layout described heterogeneous data type, and is one of the three primary things used by the compiler."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "It isn't a C++ struct, rather it's just an alias for Ptr with methods that make the get from the Col look like normal object access."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "The specific fields are: type (uint32_t), subtype (uint32_t), name (Ptr), x and y and z (floats), value (Ptr), children (Col of Ptrs), scopes (Col of Ptrs), quals (Col of Ptrs)."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "node_table (Col of Ptrs), value_table (Col of Ptrs), parent (Ptr), owner (Ptr), in_scope (Ptr), resolved and mute (bools), and opt_str (Ptr)."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "The use of these fields is all in the compiler."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "The node can theoretically be used in many places outside the compiler, though the compiler tends to grow to absorb that field rather than the node migrating."  {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Value");
            text "A Value is a thing which exists without position or behaviour of its own. It has type, data, size, and reference to other values." {post_text_theme;}
            text "A Value is almost always represented by one or more Nodes." {post_text_theme;}
            text "Value is the same sort of thing as Node in terms of storage and representation in C++ and Acorn."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "The specific fields are: type (uint32_t), subtype (uint32_t), data (Ptr), reg and loc and address (ints), size (uint32_t), subsize (uint32_t), sub_values (Col of Ptrs), quals (Col of Ptrs), type_scope (Ptr), store (Ptr)."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Same as Node, Value primarily exists in the compiler."  {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Context");
            text "A Context is the environment in which behaviour occurs, it has multiple node slots, a value, an index, a source text, and relation to other Contexts." {post_text_theme;}
            text "Context is the same sort of thing as Node and Value in terms of storage and representation in C++ and Acorn."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "The specific fields are: node and qual and left and out and root (Ptrs), result (Col of ptrs), value (Ptr), index and state and pass (ints), flag (bool), sub and parent (Ptr), source (Ptr)."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Same as Node and Value, Context primarily exists in the compiler."  {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Quals");
            text "A qual is a Node in the quals Col on either a Node or Value. They are the primary carrier of additional type behaviour and other annotations."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Like storeable types, they have three ids registered to them, the prefix id (+1) is for when they're on a Value, the suffix id (+2) is for when they're on a Node."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Firing a qual just means traversing the quals list of a Node or Value and triggering each handler with the type offset according to its position."  {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("The Triad");
            text "Together Node, Value, and Context form a triad covering most relation and behaviour I've needed to express." {post_text_theme;}
            text "They work together to represent instance, existence, and context, and the seams between them are where much of the reality in GDSL happens." {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("A Historical Aside");
            text "You may notice the actual file is called Acorn-Type, that's because the memory model started life as a way for me to avoid writing type boilerplate in a compiler." {post_text_theme;}
            text "As GDSL evolved, I realized that Type (an early system for columnar memory) was essentially a generalization of the ECS pattern which I was familiar with from my game engine work." {post_text_theme;}
            text "This was what caused GDSL to transition from being just a button scripting language to a more general purpose language. The core concept at that time was OOP syntax with ECS performance." {post_text_theme;}
            text "When GDSL was moved out of the game engine, it started to shed its core relation to Type, eventually it was pushed to the periphery as a field 'store' on Value." {post_text_theme;}
        }
    }
}



##
    register GDSL_compiler;
    "GDSL_compiler" {
        in htmlemiting {
            subemit("body",false,"","");
            string old_src = ctx.source;
            ctx.source = "<link rel='icon' type='image/png' href='/web/goldensystems/files/favicon.png'>"+old_src;
        }
    }
##

GDSL_compiler {
    standard_header;
    top_bar("/GDSL_compiler");
    div {
        outer_page_theme;
        div {
            inner_page_theme;

            div {
                "display" : "flex";
                "flex-direction" : "row";
                "justify-content" : "space-between";
                "width" : "100%";

                link "<- Core" {"href" : "/GDSL_core"; nav_arrow_theme;}
                link "Language ->" {"href" : "/GDSL_language"; nav_arrow_theme;}
            }

            section_header("Compiler");
            text "Compiler is maybe not the best term for what this is, though it's the one I used a year ago and it sticks."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "The GDSL compiler is more a nodenet assembler, annotator, and interpreter."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "It is made up of different stages and pass types. Most stages have one or two prominent functions that handle most behaviour."  {post_text_theme;}
            link "source" {"href" : "https://github.com/FirTheMouse/GDSL/blob/main/mixos-acorn/Acorn-Compiler.hpp"; "cursor" : "pointer"; post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Passes");
            text "The passes are different ways to walk the nodenet, they're actually in the Core but used almost exclusively by the Compiler at the moment."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Standard_process just invokes the handler for a type with a context."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Standard_sub_process invokes standard_process on each child of a node, it requires a context and occurs within other passes."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Standard_direct_pass is like standard_sub_process but it also traverses scopes and constructs its own context."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Standard_resolving_pass traverses scoped nodes, then unscoped nodes, then the children of scoped nodes, then the scopes of the children of nodes."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Standard_travel_pass is like the direct pass but it won't go into scopes on its own, can be passed a sub context, and can return a state."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Backwards_pass is just a backwards version of the direct pass."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Memory_backwards_pass is like the backwards pass but it processes scopes before nodes." {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("TAST");
            text "TAST means Tokenize, Assemble, Scope, Type, referring to the original four stages used to turn a source text into a nodenet ready for annotation."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Tokenizing (tokenize_stage) goes char by char down a source text stored in a Context and produces nodes from it into the Result Col on the Context, using State for things like in string behaviour."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Assembling (a_stage) goes down the Result Col and produces the actual nodenet shape from the flat stream Tokenize produced."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Naming (n_stage) was added to give some more breathing room after the a stage so names could be resolved before scoping."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Scoping (s_stage) assembles the scope relations in the nodenet."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Typing (t_stage) resolves identifiers and operators into various declarations, calls, and references."  {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("DRE");
            text "DRE means Discover, Resolve, Evaluate, referring to the original three stages used to annotate an assembled nodenet."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Discovering (d_stage) used to be an important part of identifier resolution, but all of its former responsibilities have been absorbed by the t and r stages."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Resolving (r_stage) refines relationships formed by the t stage. It used to be where all overload resolution happened, but now it’s just where constant overload resolution occurs."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Evaluating (e_stage) does dead code elimination, constant propagation, and other forms of analysis and optimization."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "The e and m stage code is currently located in the language, not the compiler, because that's how I organized things" {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("MIX");
            text "MIX means Model, Interpret, Execute, referring to the original three stages used to lower or execute an annotated nodenet."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Model (m_stage) assigns storage to all values in the nodenet, and can handle register layout and other pre-emission codegen responsibilities."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Interpret (i_stage) interprets and emits a representation of the nodent for a given target, such as turning it into ISA instructions."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Execute (x_stage) is native execution of the nodenet in GDSL, currently the main way I run code because I despise dealing with ISAs and performance hasn't been an issue." {post_text_theme;}
            link "It's worth noting most of the lowering machinery is actually located in the older GDSL-C, I never really ported over the i stage because I simply haven't needed native codegen." {"href" : "https://github.com/FirTheMouse/GDSL/blob/main/modules/GDSL-C.hpp"; "cursor" : "pointer"; post_text_theme;}
        }
    }
}


##
    register GDSL_language;
    "GDSL_language" {
        in htmlemiting {
            subemit("body",false,"","");
            string old_src = ctx.source;
            ctx.source = "<link rel='icon' type='image/png' href='/web/goldensystems/files/favicon.png'>"+old_src;
        }
    }
##

GDSL_language {
    standard_header;
    top_bar("/GDSL_language");
    div {
        outer_page_theme;
        div {
            inner_page_theme;

            div {
                "display" : "flex";
                "flex-direction" : "row";
                "justify-content" : "space-between";
                "width" : "100%";

                link "<- Compiler" {"href" : "/GDSL_compiler"; nav_arrow_theme;}
                link "Webcorn ->" {"href" : "/GDSL_webcorn"; nav_arrow_theme;}
            }

            section_header("Language");
            text "The language can be called GDSL, Acorn_script, or Q-Script, I often refer to it as Acorn_script to remove confusion with GDSL the project."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "It is really just the registration of handlers to the compiler, and the boundary between the two is quite fuzzy, don't take the way I arrange my code as gospel if you read it."  {post_text_theme;}
            link "tests" {"href" : "https://github.com/FirTheMouse/GDSL/blob/main/mixos-acorn/tests/acorntestbackup.gld"; "cursor" : "pointer"; post_text_theme;}
            link "source" {"href" : "https://github.com/FirTheMouse/GDSL/blob/main/mixos-acorn/Acorn-Script.hpp"; "cursor" : "pointer"; post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Basics");
            text "Acorn_script shares most of its basic syntax with C, brackets, braces, parens, semicolons, standard control flows, variable declarations, types, etc..."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Strings, locking, and data structures all come from the Acorn memory model rather than a standard library."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "There are various logging, debugging, and other standard library utilities baked into the language itself, since I prefer C++ interop and performance over philosophical purity at this stage." {post_text_theme;}
            text "My intent is not to make some exotic language: the syntax of C or JavaScript or Python are all proven UX, I just don't subscribe to their underlying implementations."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "I believe duck typing and high level syntax shouldn't require giving up control over memory, and that the memory model should know what's in it rather than the pointer."  {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Precompiling blocks");
            text "The two hashes (## ##), this code executes during the tokenizing stage."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Values can be passed out with the global qual, since precompiling blocks exist before any normal scopes in which a variable could be declared." {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Homoiconicity");
            text "Acorn_script is fully self modifying (including the compiler), since each handler can just be a Ptr to a scope in the langauge."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Register and newstage are keywords used to register new types and stages."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "A string with a scope is a node block, used to register behaviour via on and in which add overloads and stage behaviour respectively."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Nodes, Values, and Contexts are also native to the language, like every other data type with a layout. Inside an on or in handler the context is accessed with the ctx keyword."  {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("YAPA");
            text "YAPA means Yet Another Ptr Alias, and it's where most of the high level syntax in Acorn_script lives."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Any type which is really just an interpretation of Ptr can be registered as a YAPA type with a level, this gives it overloads telling it which level of its Ptr is the important one."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "As an example: a YAPA-1 Ptr refers to a Col, the important field is slot (sidx). If you called .get on a YAPA-1 you would move the slot. A YAPA-2 refers to a ColCol, .get would give you a YAPA-1 Ptr by moving the column (idx) field."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "YAPAs can also be indirect, meaning they are a YAPA-1 of Ptrs which should be resolved an additional time on gets and such. Object is one of these, which is why it can have different properties without a layout."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Locking, database operations, and general data structure work all go through the YAPA interface."  {post_text_theme;}
        }
    }
}


##
    register GDSL_webcorn;
    "GDSL_webcorn" {
        in htmlemiting {
            subemit("body",false,"","");
            string old_src = ctx.source;
            ctx.source = "<link rel='icon' type='image/png' href='/web/goldensystems/files/favicon.png'>"+old_src;
        }
    }
##

GDSL_webcorn {
    standard_header;
    top_bar("/GDSL_webcorn");
    div {
        outer_page_theme;
        div {
            inner_page_theme;

            div {
                "display" : "flex";
                "flex-direction" : "row";
                "justify-content" : "space-between";
                "width" : "100%";

                link "<- Language" {"href" : "/GDSL_language"; nav_arrow_theme;}
                link "TwigSnap ->" {"href" : "/GDSL_twigsnap"; nav_arrow_theme;}
            }

            section_header("Webcorn");
            text "Webcorn is the networking layer for GDSL and contains all the faculties and systems used to host servers and serve webpages."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "It also has a lot of code I should probably delete because it isn't used anymore."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "A lot of the important stuff belongs in TwigSnap's page, so this one is probably going to be quite brief."  {post_text_theme;}
            link "source" {"href" : "https://github.com/FirTheMouse/Webcorn/blob/main/web/Webcorn-Core.hpp"; "cursor" : "pointer"; post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Basics");
            text "All the normal POSIX, tls provided by Embedtls, exposed as Acorn_script functions."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Cry is one of the IPC methods, a unit can mark itself dead and expose its label, a session manager unit polls and services such crying units."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Units can be dispatched, with a server created to keep track of them, though this way of doing things has been pushed to the side by the newer stateless model."  {post_text_theme;}
       
            section_header("Webcorn.gld");
            text "The actual Acorn_script code that handles starting up the units, tls, and dispatching received requests."  {post_text_theme;}
        }
    }
}


##
    register GDSL_twigsnap;
    "GDSL_twigsnap" {
        in htmlemiting {
            subemit("body",false,"","");
            string old_src = ctx.source;
            ctx.source = "<link rel='icon' type='image/png' href='/web/goldensystems/files/favicon.png'>"+old_src;
        }
    }
##

GDSL_twigsnap {
    standard_header;
    top_bar("/GDSL_twigsnap");
    div {
        outer_page_theme;
        div {
            inner_page_theme;

            div {
                "display" : "flex";
                "flex-direction" : "row";
                "justify-content" : "space-between";
                "width" : "100%";

                link "<- Webcorn" {"href" : "/GDSL_webcorn"; nav_arrow_theme;}
                link "GDSL ->" {"href" : "/GDSL"; nav_arrow_theme;}
            }


            section_header("TwigSnap");
            text "TwigSnap is a dialect of Acorn_script that introduces html emission behaviour, request handeling, and the RUN protocal."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "It's what I program websites like this in." {post_text_theme;}
            link "source" {"href" : "https://github.com/FirTheMouse/Webcorn/blob/main/web/webrunner.gld"; "cursor" : "pointer"; post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Basics");
            text "TwigSnap has the same syntax as Acorn_script, but introduces new scoped keywords like div, text, input, which resolve as function calls."  {post_text_theme;}
            link "I could explain it in depth, but I feel like just looking at the code for this website is more informative." {"href" : "https://github.com/FirTheMouse/Webcorn/blob/main/web/goldensystems/website.twg"; "cursor" : "pointer"; post_text_theme;}
            link "Or my current project if you want a more complete and complex application." {"href" : "https://github.com/FirTheMouse/Webcorn/blob/main/web/electionmadness/website.twg"; "cursor" : "pointer"; post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Subemit");
            text "TwigSnap emits HTML by running an htmlemitting stage, each scoped keyword uses subemit to traverse its children and construct a stack of properties."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Since TwigSnap is just Acorn_script but more, all normal variables, functions, and control flows still work during this stage. If a type has no htmlemitting handler it defaults to its x stage handler."  {post_text_theme;}
            
            section_header("Inlined");
            text "Inlined is a TwigSnap keyword that essentially just makes a function call, at this stage there's no real difference between void and inlined, but in the future I can add optimizations here."  {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Template");
            text "A function call which can preserve its scope, if it isn't declared as stateless. This was more prominent before I started liking statless more."  {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("Capture");
            text "A keyword which emits a string pointing to itself and a list of its arguments as strings. It can also be signaled to instead run itself when called like a function call."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Because nodes have a persistent identity they can be serialized and called back, and even preserved across restarts by having the subunit re-adopt the Ptr."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "Captures can also execute JavaScript code on the client."  {post_text_theme;}

            section_header("RUN protocal");
            text "This is what captures emit as strings, on the TwigSnap end it is decoded into a Node to execute like a function call with arguments, on the JavaScript side it can be interpreted as fragment swaps and code to eval."  {post_text_theme;}
            text "I prefer to push behaviour into JavaScript on the client to save roundtripping when it's a presentation concern. Though TwigSnap can very much operate server-side."  {post_text_theme;}
        }
    }
}






